Package: fstx
Type: Package
Title: Fast, Safe Column-Store Files for R Data Frames
Version: 0.99.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Harsh", "Chourasia", role = c("aut", "cre"), email = "fstx-maintainer@example.invalid"),
    person("Meta Platforms, Inc. and affiliates", role = "cph", comment = "bundled 'Zstandard' library"),
    person("Yann Collet", role = "cph", comment = "bundled 'LZ4' and 'xxHash' libraries"),
    person("Google Inc.", role = "cph", comment = "bundled 'FlatBuffers' runtime headers"),
    person("CWI, TU Munich, FSU Jena", role = "cph", comment = "bundled 'FSST' library"),
    person("The Highway Project Authors", role = "cph", comment = "bundled 'Highway' library"))
Description: Reads and writes data frames in a column-store file format built
    for speed and safety. Each column is split into 64-byte aligned pages, and
    every page gets the lightweight encoding that fits it best (bit-packing,
    frame-of-reference, delta, run-length, dictionary, 'ALP' for doubles,
    'FSST' for strings), optionally under 'LZ4' or 'Zstandard' compression.
    A 'FlatBuffers' footer holds a page directory with per-page statistics,
    and every page carries an 'xxHash' checksum. Reads and writes run in
    parallel, with SIMD decode kernels from 'Highway' chosen at run time.
    Supports lazy 'ALTREP' columns over the memory-mapped file, character
    columns whose R strings are made on first use, factors built from string
    dictionaries, partial reads by row list, filters that skip pages by their
    statistics, crash-safe appends and compaction. The file format (fstx
    format 1.0) is written down in a specification shipped with the package,
    and files written by 'fstx' 0.7 are read as they are.
License: MIT + file LICENSE
Encoding: UTF-8
Depends: R (>= 4.4.0)
LinkingTo: cpp11
Suggests: testthat (>= 3.0.0), data.table, tibble, dplyr, nanoarrow
Config/testthat/edition: 3
SystemRequirements: C++17
